<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leo III the Isaurian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/717">717</a>–741 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Leo III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leo III the Isaurian was a Byzantine emperor who reigned from 717 to 741 and founded the Isaurian dynasty. He successfully defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> against the great Arab siege of 717 to 718, a decisive moment in the survival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. He is also associated with the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-iconoclasm">Iconoclast controversy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leo-iii-the-isaurian.jpg" alt="Leo III the Isaurian" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidus_of_Leo_III_sb1504.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leo was born about 685 at Germanikeia, in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, with the name Konon, and spoke Arabic as his native language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor">Theophanes the Confessor</a> called him Saracen-minded, though there is little evidence that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> influenced him. Most modern scholars reject the nickname the Isaurian as a misnomer, perhaps arising when authors mistook his home town for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cankiri">Germanicopolis</a> in Isauria. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-ii">Justinian II</a> he was sent on a mission to Alania and Lazica; Emperor Anastasius II later made him commander of the Anatolic Theme.</p><p>He then joined Artabasdos, commander of the Armeniac Theme, in a plot against Emperor Theodosius III, ostensibly in support of Anastasius. In 717 he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, forced Theodosius to abdicate, and was crowned by Patriarch Germanus I at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> on 25 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Leo at once faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-arab-siege-of-constantinople">second Arab siege</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, which began in August 717. Umayyad forces of 80,000 to 150,000 men and a large fleet had come to seize the city. Preparations begun under Anastasius II, Leo's resistance, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire">Greek fire</a> and Bulgarian reinforcements wore the invaders down, and they abandoned the siege in August 718 after twelve months. He then reorganised the administration, put down a rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and executed the deposed Anastasius II, who tried to retake the throne. He invited Slavic settlers into depopulated districts, restored the army, and allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>. Raids continued until a major invading force was destroyed at the Battle of Akroinon in 740.</p><p>After enforcing the baptism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Montanists in 722, Leo issued edicts against the veneration of images between 726 and 729. Patriarch Germanus resigned in 730 rather than accept iconoclasm. Popes Gregory II and Gregory III opposed it, and Leo transferred bishoprics of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and Illyricum from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Some historians, notably Leslie Brubaker and John Haldon, doubt that Leo's policy involved mass persecution or destruction of images. His Ekloge, a revision of Justinian's code published in 741, replaced the death penalty with mutilation in many cases. In 721 he introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> miliaresion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leo died of dropsy on 18 June 741 and was buried in the Church of the Holy Apostles. His son Constantine V succeeded him. The surviving sources were mostly written by iconodule clergy hostile to him; modern historians describe his reign as a period of violence that saved the Empire from destruction amid extensive reform.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leo III the Isaurian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the second Arab siege of 717–718, <b>Emperor Leo III</b> used Greek fire to devastate the Umayyad fleet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Synaxarion ties the feast to the year 626, when Constantinople, in the reign of Heraclius, was attacked by the Persians and the Avars and was saved, it says, by the Theotokos; it also credits her with two later victories, under <b>Leo III the Isaurian</b> and Constantine Pogonatus.</blockquote><a href="/songs/akathist">Akathist Hymn</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Emperor Leo III</b>'s defenders had been helped by Greek fire, a harsh winter and Bulgar attacks.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-15">15 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Byzantine emperor Theodosius III abdicated in favour of <b>Leo III the Isaurian</b>, the general who had captured his son.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-25">25 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His defiance of the Byzantine emperor <b>Leo III the Isaurian</b> in the iconoclastic controversy eventually led to the establishment of the popes' temporal power.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-19">19 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Disputes with Constantinople over doctrine and taxation, especially Iconoclasm under <b>Leo III</b>, loosened ties to the empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-rome">Duchy of Rome</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahramanmaras">Kahramanmaraş</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.59,36.95&amp;year=729" aria-label="Kahramanmaraş on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5473 811 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leo III the Isaurian"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5923 1081h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5923 1081h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.59,36.95&amp;year=729">What was here in 729 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
